## 2. Configure SSH Login

### 2.1 Generate an SSH key pair (recommended over passwords)

In PowerShell:

```powershell
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your_email@example.com"
```

- Press **Enter** to accept the default location: `C:\Users\<you>\.ssh\id_ed25519`
- Optionally set a passphrase (recommended).

This creates:
- `id_ed25519` — **private key** (keep secret, never share)
- `id_ed25519.pub` — **public key** (safe to copy to servers)

### 2.2 Copy your public key to the remote server

PowerShell does not include `ssh-copy-id`, so use this one-liner:

```powershell
type $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub | ssh user@remote-host "mkdir -p ~/.ssh && chmod 700 ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"
```

Replace `user@remote-host` with your username and server address. Enter your password once when prompted. Subsequent logins will use the key.

### 2.3 Create an SSH config file

Create or edit `C:\Users\<you>\.ssh\config` (no extension). This lets you define named hosts, jump hosts, and options.

```sshconfig
# ~/.ssh/config

Host myserver
    HostName 10.0.0.42
    User jung
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
    ForwardAgent yes
    ServerAliveInterval 60
    ServerAliveCountMax 3

# Example: connecting through a bastion / jump host
Host internal-server
    HostName 192.168.1.100
    User jung
    ProxyJump bastion.example.com
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
```

> **Tip:** `ServerAliveInterval` keeps idle SSH sessions from dropping — useful for long remote dev sessions.

### 2.4 Test the connection

```powershell
ssh myserver
```

You should land on the remote shell without a password prompt (a passphrase prompt for the key is normal if you set one).

