## 5. Install Extensions on the Remote Host

VS Code extensions run in one of two places:

- **UI (local)** — themes, keymaps: stay on Windows.
- **Workspace (remote)** — language servers, linters, debuggers: must be installed **on the remote host**.

To install a remote extension:

1. While connected via SSH, open the **Extensions** view.
2. Extensions already installed locally show **Install in SSH: myserver**. Click it.
3. New extensions install to the remote by default while connected.

To make extensions install automatically on every remote host, add to your **remote** `settings.json`:

```jsonc
{
  "remote.SSH.defaultExtensions": [
    "ms-python.python",
    "ms-vscode.cpptools",
    "github.copilot",
    "github.copilot-chat"
  ]
}
```

> **Note:** GitHub Copilot and Copilot Chat must be enabled on the remote side to use AI features against remote code.

